{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789820676,"nonce":"Sltp3/vR4MMeYKUJGR3tzP7gw/g=","title":"SSA: a variable read through a block not yet wired resolves to undef","message":"After IDF placement, a read in an unsealed block with no predecessors yet\n(a loop body block read while its header's branch is still being built)\nreturned undef. The recursive read of a loop-invariant variable from the\nheader walks the latch back up the body to that block and takes the undef\nas the latch's contribution, so `n = len(ys)` before a loop became\n`phi [n, undef]` at the header. With the undef filtered out of the\nuniqueness test this collapsed to `n` by luck; with undefs counted it was\na real phi and the interpreter read Undef on the second iteration\n(\"type mismatch: expected integer, got Undef\" in zb_list_lt_i8, and the\nsame in any function with an invariant read after a nested branch).\n\nFix: such a block gets an incomplete phi to hold the place, filled when\nevery edge exists; `fill_incomplete_phis` loops until no phi is left,\nsince filling one can add another; trivial phis (all incomings one value\nor the phi itself) are then collapsed, chains resolved within a round,\nbecause the ownership pass reads a phi chain as a value of its own to\nrelease. `substitute_value` moves the recorded uses along, so the def-use\nchains built afterwards do not name a value that no longer exists.","files":null}]}